					<time datetime="2021-01-09T20:49:46-06:00">
						January 9, 2021 at 8:49 pm					</time>
				</p>
			</div>
				<p>Manu, I&#8217;m not Kris but hopefully it can help:</p>
<p>1. 30% is for each stock. Meaning you can have 10k, with 3k in 3 stocks and 1k in another. But not any single position is more than 30% of your total account.</p>
<p>2. The max risk you calculate it per share risk, then from there you calculate the number of total shares you to trade, capping it with your over-the-top rule (of never more than 30%). </p>
<p>So again supposing you&#8217;ve a 10k account, risking 1% per trade (100$), it means if you enter in a stock at 10$, and your stop is 9.50$ (50c), then you can risk (RISK/PER SHARE RISK)=100/0.5= 200shares at 10$ = 2k position</p>
<p>If your stop would be only 20cents instead, following these rules, you wouldn&#8217;t take 500 shares position, because it would be 5k and 50% of your account it (unless for some reasons you&#8217;re extremely confident about it etc and can stomach the risks). But you would cap it to 300 shares = 3k = 30% of account</p>
<p>Hope it clarifies it!</p>
